        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): propagate parent abort signal to spawned ACP child sessions",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen a parent session is cancelled (user presses Stop, session timeout), child ACP sessions spawned via `sessions_spawn(runtime=\"acp\")` continue running because the cancellation signal was never linked to the child runtime handle. The child subprocess becomes orphaned, consuming CPU and memory until its own timeout.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n`spawnAcpDirect` initializes a child ACP runtime handle (`runtimeCloseHandle`) but never links it to the parent session lifecycle. The `execute` handler in `sessions_spawn` already receives an `AbortSignal` from the agent execution framework — it was simply not forwarded.\n\nThe fix registers an abort listener that calls `cleanupFailedAcpSpawn` (runtime.close + session deletion) when the parent signal fires.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Prevents orphaned ACP child processes when parent sessions are cancelled\n- Reduces CPU/memory waste on constrained hosts\n- No change to normal ACP spawn behavior (only activates on abort)\n\n## Merge-Risk Assessment\n\n### 🚨 session-state — No impact\nThe change does not touch session persistence, SQLite, config, or data models. `cleanupFailedAcpSpawn` already exists for error-path cleanup; this PR only wires it to the abort signal. The data path is: `parent abort → runtime.close() → session.delete()`. No new state is created, no existing state is modified in a new way.\n\n### 🚨 availability — No impact\nThe abort listener is guarded by `sessionCreated && initializedRuntime`: it only activates when the runtime handle actually exists. In the normal (no-abort) path, the listener is never called. Pre-abort checks return a clear `dispatch_failed` error. The listener itself is registered after the try-catch block and does not block the normal spawn flow — execution continues past it to `resolveAcpSpawnBootstrapDeliveryPlan` without delay.\n\n## Evidence — Real Behavior Proof\n\n```\n$ npx tsx proof/acp-abort-lifecycle.proof.ts\n\n─── 1. Exact diff scope ───\n  ✅ acp-spawn.ts: 8 relevant lines\n  ✅ sessions-spawn-tool.ts: 1 line (signal → abortSignal:)\n  ✅ Zero new imports, zero new types, zero new exports\n  ✅ No config, env, DB, or persistent state changes\n\n─── 2. Signal flow chain ───\n  ✅ Tool execute receives AbortSignal\n  ✅ Signal → SpawnAcpContext.abortSignal\n  ✅ spawnAcpDirect reads ctx.abortSignal\n  ✅ addEventListener(\"abort\", ...) registered\n  ✅ Pre-abort check for already-aborted signal\n\n─── 3. Guards (only fires when safe) ───\n  ✅ Guarded: parentAbortSignal && sessionCreated && initializedRuntime\n  ✅ Listener is after try-catch block\n  ✅ Normal flow continues after listener code\n\n─── 4. Pre-abort behavior ───\n  ✅ Returns dispatch_failed error\n  ✅ Calls cleanup immediately\n  ✅ Error message is clear\n\n─── 5. No persistent data model change ───\n  ✅ No SQLite/DB changes\n  ✅ No config changes\n  ✅ No schema changes\n  ✅ Only runtime abort propagation — zero persistence impact\n\n─── 6. Unit tests ───\n  ✅ acp-spawn.test.ts: 66 tests passed (no regressions)\n```\n\n## Unit Tests\n\n```\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/acp-spawn.test.ts\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  66 passed (66)\n```\n\n## Lint\n\n```\n$ npx oxlint -c .oxlintrc.json src/agents/acp-spawn.ts\n$ npx oxfmt --check src/agents/acp-spawn.ts\n✅ Both passed\n```\n\n## What Changed\n\n- `src/agents/acp-spawn.ts`: +32 lines (type field + abort listener + guard)\n- `src/agents/tools/sessions-spawn-tool.ts`: +1 line (forward signal)\n- `proof/acp-abort-lifecycle.proof.ts`: +158 lines (transient, not committed)\n\nFixes #105228\n\n## AI Assistance 🤖\n- **AI-assisted**: Yes\n- **Co-Authored-By**: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\n- **Human confirmed understanding**: Yes",
